NOT covered by the Apache licence:

  models/chips_model.pt and models/drinks_model.pt were trained with
  Ultralytics YOLOv8, which is AGPL-3.0. Those weights are AGPL-derived, and so
  is any program that loads them together with ultralytics.

  Two places load them. Experiment E1
  (research/experiments.py:e1_closed_set_baseline) uses them as the closed-set
  baseline and as an auto-labelling tool. Since 24 September 2026 the till can
  also be switched into "Closed-set (v1)" mode, which runs them through
  recognition/closed_set.py:ClosedSetRecogniser so the two approaches can be
  demonstrated side by side.

  Read this plainly: WHEN THAT MODE IS SELECTED THE RUNNING PROGRAM IS NOT
  APACHE-2.0. AGPL-3.0 applies to it, including over a network. A distribution
  of this project that is intended to be Apache-2.0 must ship neither the
  weights nor ultralytics; without them the mode reports itself unavailable and
  the till runs entirely on the retrieval recogniser, which is Apache-2.0 and is
  the default.

  What keeps that separation real: nothing imports ultralytics at module scope,
  so a till that never selects the mode never loads it
  (tests/test_no_ultralytics.py enforces the import boundary); the weights are
  never committed (.gitignore); and ultralytics stays in
  requirements-research.txt, not requirements.txt.

  Do not ship the weights inside a proprietary product.

